## 4. More passes mean more cycle time and cost

Every extra patterning pass can require some combination of:

- another mask;
- another resist coat and bake;
- another scanner exposure;
- another development step;
- another etch or deposition step;
- extra cleaning;
- extra metrology;
- and more waiting in tool queues.

The exact flow depends on the patterning scheme, but the economic direction is clear.

A layer that needs two, three or four patterning cycles consumes more tool time than a layer printed once.

That raises wafer cost and lengthens cycle time even if every individual step works perfectly.

## 5. Self-aligned patterning changes the trick

Not all multiple patterning simply uses several masks.

In **self-aligned double patterning**, or SADP, the fab first prints a relatively easy template. It then deposits a thin spacer material along the sides of that template and removes selected material so the spacers themselves define a denser set of features.

The clever part is that the new lines are partly defined by deposition thickness rather than by asking a second optical exposure to position every line independently.

Self-aligned quadruple patterning, or SAQP, repeats the idea to create even denser structures.

This can reduce some overlay sensitivity for the repeated line pattern, but it adds deposition, etch and process-control steps.

So again, the bottleneck migrates rather than vanishes.

## 6. Why NAND is an important contrast

Leading-edge logic often fights to create very small horizontal pitches.

Modern NAND took a different route: instead of continuing to shrink every memory cell only in two dimensions, manufacturers began stacking many layers vertically.

That does not eliminate difficult patterning, but it changes where complexity accumulates. Very deep etch, deposition uniformity and vertical structure control become central.

This is a useful expert habit: when one scaling method becomes too expensive, the industry may change architecture rather than keep repeating the same process more times forever.

## 7. Why EUV changed the economics

EUV's shorter wavelength allowed some critical layers that previously needed several DUV patterning steps to be printed with fewer exposures.

That can remove masks, process steps, queue time and overlay opportunities.

This is why comparing lithography tools only by purchase price is incomplete.

An EUV scanner is extremely expensive, but its economic value can include replacing several DUV exposures and the deposition, etch, cleaning and metrology work surrounding them.

The correct comparison is often **cost and good-wafer output for the full patterning flow**, not scanner price versus scanner price.

